#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3262839-4028-5d90-bf8a-cb4de958813a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279523} {:answer/id #uuid "0e326d50-6309-5e63-ba00-a5eb1f4e96b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e326d50-6309-5e63-ba00-a5eb1f4e96b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279524} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d153166-8679-52de-a7b3-11c4606fe3fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39eba7b4-55f1-5b6e-bfe4-784dcc986294" :question/math-academy-id "q-319437" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b92df9bf-57f8-52c4-bdb1-778f42842a6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288574} {:answer/id #uuid "30d4d4f3-edf3-50e0-b558-676c2c519051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} {:answer/id #uuid "88d0c1ad-2e69-53e8-b0f1-14231d6b6c48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288576} {:answer/id #uuid "104c5573-009e-55dc-872f-3406140f4ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288577} {:answer/id #uuid "42c0d506-1aa1-50f1-925f-3776c434998d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d4d4f3-edf3-50e0-b558-676c2c519051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288575} :answer-field/id #uuid "32185872-743f-5625-abaf-14cc3904b386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b53bfca7-a35b-5bdd-8a26-779771b1f95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9980" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{2}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}4 & k \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(k-2)-3(4-6)+1(4-3k) \\\\  & =2k-4+6+4-3k \\\\  & =6-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(4-6) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-k}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{2}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{6-k} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =2(6-k) \\\\ 10 & =12-2k \\\\ 2k & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
